(2894968, 404795, 63398, '<td valign="top">Nice shot, put together in a very classy way, certainly in keeping with the more cultured market of Guiness rather than the halftime/beach party aspect that most beers go for in their ads. <br/> <br/>The spaghetti strap suggests that timeless "little black dress" which suggests to me that this is a totally gorgeous woman aesthetically pleasing to the eye as well as having the discriminating palate for the Guiness.<br/> <br/>Though by and large I find using beautiful women to sell things is chauvinistic, this is done so tastefully I can forgive it.<br/> <br/>The lighting, choice of dress color, her hair, her eyes, the body of the Guinness all work beautifully with her skin tone to really make a terrific ad.<br/> <br/>The only thing I could find that I could not totally sign off on is the weird reflection that almost looks like an "N" above and to the left of the "G" in Guinness.<br/> <br/>Other than that, it''s nigh onto a perfect shot in my book for photo quality and composition.<br/> <br/>Challenge met!</td>', 'Nice shot, put together in a very classy way, certainly in keeping with the more cultured market of Guiness rather than the halftime/beach party aspect that most beers go for in their ads.  The spaghetti strap suggests that timeless "little black dress" which suggests to me that this is a totally gorgeous woman aesthetically pleasing to the eye as well as having the discriminating palate for the Guiness. Though by and large I find using beautiful women to sell things is chauvinistic, this is done so tastefully I can forgive it. The lighting, choice of dress color, her hair, her eyes, the body of the Guinness all work beautifully with her skin tone to really make a terrific ad. The only thing I could find that I could not totally sign off on is the weird reflection that almost looks like an "N" above and to the left of the "G" in Guinness. Other than that, it''s nigh onto a perfect shot in my book for photo quality and composition. Challenge met!', '2006-10-04 09:15:00.000000', NULL, 1),
